<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jacques Cartier</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1491">1491</a>–<a href="/years/1557">1557</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jacques Cartier was a French explorer who made voyages to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> from 1534. He explored the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-st-lawrence">Gulf of St. Lawrence</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-lawrence-river">St. Lawrence River</a> and claimed the region for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. His expeditions are regarded as the beginning of French claims in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> and part of the foundation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jacques-cartier.jpg" alt="Jacques Cartier" width="313" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Théophile Hamel / After François Nicholas Riss · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jacques_Cartier_1851-1852.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Jacques Cartier was born on 31 December 1491 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-malo">Saint-Malo</a>, the port on the north-east coast of Brittany. A respected mariner, he improved his social standing in 1520 by marrying Mary Catherine des Granches, a member of a leading aristocratic family. In 1534 Jean Le Veneur, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Saint-Malo, introduced him to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i-duke-of-nevers">Francis I</a>, citing voyages to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newfoundland">Newfoundland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> as proof of his ability to lead ships to new lands.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Cartier sailed on 20 April 1534 under a royal commission, hoping to find a western passage to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">East Indies</a>. He explored the coasts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newfoundland">Newfoundland</a>, the Gaspé and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-st-lawrence">Gulf of St. Lawrence</a>, and on 24 July planted a 10-metre cross in Gaspé Bay to claim the land for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. There he kidnapped the two sons of the St. Lawrence Iroquoian chief Donnacona, who agreed on condition that they return with European goods.</p><p>On a second voyage in 1535 he sailed up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-lawrence-river">St. Lawrence</a> with three ships and 110 men to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stadacona">Stadacona</a>, and on 2 October reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hochelaga">Hochelaga</a>, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a>, where over a thousand people greeted the French. The rapids blocked his way; convinced that the river was the Northwest Passage, he named them the Lachine Rapids after the French word for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. The fleet wintered frozen at the mouth of the St. Charles River, and scurvy killed about 50 Iroquoians. A remedy from the tree annedda, learned from Domagaya, saved 85 of the French. Cartier then took Donnacona to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> to tell of the Kingdom of Saguenay, said to hold gold and rubies.</p><p>In 1541 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-francois-de-la-rocque-de-roberval">Jean-François de La Rocque de Roberval</a> was named lieutenant general of the colonisation project, with Cartier as chief navigator. Cartier sailed ahead with five ships and settled at Cap-Rouge, founding the fortified Charlesbourg-Royal. The minerals he took to be diamonds and gold proved to be quartz and iron pyrites. Iroquoians reportedly killed about 35 settlers during the winter, and he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1542; the settlement was abandoned in 1543.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Cartier spent his last years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-malo">Saint-Malo</a> and on his nearby estate, and died on 1 September 1557 at the age of 65 during an epidemic, possibly of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/typhus">typhus</a>, and was buried in Saint-Malo Cathedral. He was the first European to describe and map the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-st-lawrence">Gulf of St. Lawrence</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-lawrence-river">St. Lawrence River</a>, and the first to document the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, which comes from the Iroquoian word kanata, meaning village. Eastern parts of the continent had been visited before him by the Norse and by Basque, Galician and Breton fishermen, and no permanent European settlement followed before 1605.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jacques Cartier” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Jacques Cartier</b> reached the Gulf of St. Lawrence in 1534, claiming territory for Francis I, but decades of religious civil war prevented meaningful colonisation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-empire">French Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>France’s claim in North America began in 1534, when <b>Jacques Cartier</b>, sailing for Francis I, explored the Gulf of St Lawrence and planted a cross on the Gaspé Peninsula.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-france">New France · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Jacques Cartier</b> left Brittany on the first of three voyages that explored the Gulf of St Lawrence.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-20">20 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Jacques Cartier</b> raised a nine-metre wooden cross at Gaspé Bay bearing the arms of the King of France.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-24">24 July in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Rothéneuf, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Rothéneuf, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.68,-1.97&amp;year=1533" aria-label="Rothéneuf on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4504 463 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Jacques Cartier"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4954 733h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4954 733h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.68,-1.97&amp;year=1533">What was here in 1533? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
